And so, he sold some of his slaves. He said to them, "Go to those who have been invited to the feast, and say to them, 'I have prepared a feast, and I have killed my fattened oxen. All is ready. Come to the feast.'" **Explanation of Translation Choices:** * **"وبعد، بعت شوية من عبيده."** - Translated as "And so, he sold some of his slaves." * "وبعد" is a common Arabic introductory phrase, often translated as "And so," "Therefore," or "Now." * "بعت شوية من عبيده" is a straightforward statement of selling some of his slaves. "شوية" means "some" or "a few." * **"قال لهم، 'قولوا للمدعوين اللي عملت عشا، ذبحت بقري وابقاري السمانة. كل شيء جاهز. تعالوا لعشا العرس.'"** - Translated as "He said to them, 'Go to those who have been invited to the feast, and say to them, 'I have prepared a feast, and I have killed my fattened oxen. All is ready. Come to the feast.'" * This is a longer, more complex sentence, containing a direct speech quote within the larger narrative. * "قال لهم" - "He said to them." This is a standard way of introducing direct speech in Arabic narrative. * The core of the instruction is the command to relay a specific message to the invited guests. This message details the preparations for the feast, the killing of the fattened oxen (a sign of a grand celebration), and the invitation for the guests to attend the feast. * The use of the present perfect tense ("have prepared," "have killed") in the English translation effectively conveys the sense of completed actions in preparation for the feast, with the invitations being sent out as the final step before the event itself.
