And he said, "Are you going to destroy the temple and rebuild it in three days? Show yourself! Get down from the cross, if you are the son of God!"
In the same way, the chief priests, the teachers of the law and the elders mocked him.
They said, "He saved others," but they said, "As for himself, he cannot save! He is the King of Israel! Let him come down now from the cross! Then we will believe in him."
He trusts in God. Let God save him now if he loves him. He's the one who said, "I am the son of God."
In the same way, the thieves who were with Jesus on the cross also mocked him. Jesus dies
From noon until three o'clock, the entire earth was covered in darkness.
About the third hour, Jesus cried out with a loud voice, saying, "Eloi, Eloi, lama sabachthani?" which means "My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?" (Psalm 22:1)
Some of those standing there, when they heard Jesus cry out, said, "He is calling for Elijah."
Immediately, one of them ran to fetch a sponge; he filled it with sour wine and put it on a stick; he held it up to Jesus' lips so that he could drink.
The others said, "Let him alone. Let's see if Elijah will come to save him."
After Jesus cried out again in a loud voice, he died.
At that moment, the temple curtain was rent from top to bottom. The earth quaked. The stones split.
Tombs were shattered. The bodies of many holy people who died rose from the dead.
They came out of the tombs. After Jesus rose from the dead, they went to the holy city. There they appeared to many people.
The Roman leader and the soldiers guarding Jesus saw the earthquake and all that happened. They were terrified. They exclaimed, "Truly, he is the Son of God!"
It was not far, many women were watching. They followed Jesus from Galilee to care for his needs.
Mary Magdalene was among them. Mary, the mother of James and Joseph, was also present. She was also the mother of the sons of Zebedee. Jesus is buried
As the evening approached, a rich man from the town of Arimathea came. His name was Joseph. He became a disciple of Jesus.
He went to Pilate and asked for the body of Jesus. Pilate ordered that it be given to him.
Joseph took the body and wrapped it in clean linen cloth.
He put it in his new grave which he had carved out of the rock. He rolled a large stone in front of the entrance to the grave. Then he left.
Mary Magdalene and the other Mary were sitting there opposite the tomb. The guards at the tomb
The next day, after the day of preparation, the leading priests and the Pharisees went to Paul.
Sir, they said, "We remember something the liar said while he was still alive. He said, 'After three days, I will rise again.'"
Give the order to protect the tomb until the third day. If you don't, his disciples might come and steal the body. And then they would tell the people that Jesus rose from the dead. This final lie would be even worse than the first one.
"Take some guards with you," Pilate said. "Go. Make the tomb as secure as you can."
They went and made the tomb secure. They put a seal on it and appointed guards on duty.
Jesus rose from the dead.
The Sabbath rest was now past. It was the dawn of the first day of the week. Mary Magdalene and the other Mary went to look at the tomb.
There was a strong earthquake. An angel of the Lord descended from heaven. The angel walked to the tomb. He turned the stone and sat on it.
His body was shining like lightning. His clothes were white as snow.
The guards were so scared of him that they trembled and became like the dead.
The angel said to the women, "Do not be afraid. I know that you are looking for Jesus, who was crucified."
He's not here! He got up, just as he said he would! Come and see the place where he was lying.
Run quickly! Say to his disciples, 'He has been raised from the dead. He will go ahead of you into Galilee. There you will see him.' Now I have told you.'
The women hurried from the tomb, for they were afraid yet filled with joy. They ran to tell the disciples.
Suddenly, Jesus found them. He said to them, "Peace!" They came to Him, touched His feet and worshiped Him.
Then Jesus said to them, "Do not be afraid. Go and tell them to return to her, to Galilee. There they will see me." The guards report to the chief priests.
And as we women were walking away, some of the guards entered the city. They reported everything that had happened to the high priests.
When the elder statesmen met with the senators, they came up with a plan. They gave the soldiers a large sum of money.
They told the soldiers, "We like you to say, 'His disciples came at night. They stole his body while we were sleeping.'"
If the Sultan hears this report, we will pay him off. This way you will get out of trouble.
The soldiers took the money and did as they were told. This story spread among the Jews until this day. Jesus's last commands to his disciples.
And so, the eleven disciples went to Galilee. They went to the mountain that Jesus had told them to go to.
When they saw it, they worshipped it. However, some of them still had doubts.
Then Jesus came to them. He said to them, "All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me."
You must go and teach all nations. Baptize them in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit.
Teach them to obey everything I have ordered you. And you can be sure that I am always with you, to the end.
John the Baptist is preparing the way.
This is the beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God.
A long time ago, the prophet Isaiah wrote, "I send my messenger before you; he will prepare your way." (Malachi 3:1)
A messenger calls in the desert, "Prepare the way for the Lord. Make straight paths for him." (Isaiah 40:3)
And so John came. He baptized people in the desert. And he further called on people to be baptized and to turn away from their sins. And then God would forgive them.
All the people from the countryside of Judea went out to him. All the people from Jerusalem also went out. When they confessed that they had sinned, John baptized them in the river Jordan.
John is wearing a garment made of camel hair. He has a leather belt, a belt on his waist. And he ate locusts and wild honey.
Here's the translation: Hani, I'm going to tell you what John used to preach. "After me, someone will come who is more powerful than I am. I am not worthy to stoop down and untie His sandals."
He baptizes you with water; but he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit." Jesus is baptized and tempted.
At that time, Jesus came from Nazareth in Galilee. John baptized him in the River Jordan.
Jesus was coming up out of the water. At that moment, he saw the heavens being torn open. He saw the Holy Spirit descending on him like a dove.
A voice spoke to him from heaven. It said, "You are my son, and I love you. I am so pleased with you."
In the meantime, the Holy Spirit sent Jesus into the wilderness.
He was in the desert for 40 days. Satan tempted him. Wild beasts did not harm Jesus. Angels took care of him. Jesus chose the first disciples.
After John was imprisoned, Jesus went to Galilee. He preached the good news of God.
The time has come, he said. The Kingdom of God is near. Turn away from your sins and believe the good news!
One day, Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee. There, he saw Simon and his brother Andrew. They were throwing a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
Come. Follow me, said Jesus. "I will make you fishers of men."
Meanwhile, they left their window and followed him.
And after that, Jesus walked a little further. As he did, he saw James, the son of Zebedee, and his brother John. They were in a boat mending their nets.
Immediately he called them. Leave your father Zebedee in the boat with the hired men. And follow me. Jesus Expels an Evil Spirit
Jesus and those with Him went to Capernaum. When the Sabbath came, He entered the synagogue. There He began to teach.
People were astonished at his teaching. He taught them as one having authority. He did not teach like the teachers of the law.
Suddenly a man in their synagogue cried out. An evil spirit had control over him. He said,
What do you want from us, Jesus of Nazareth? Did you come to destroy us? I know who you are. You are the Holy One from God!
Be quiet! Jesus said firmly. Get out of him!
The evil spirit violently shook the man. Then it came out of him with a scream.
Everyone was amazed and began to ask one another, "What is this? A new teaching, with authority! Even the unclean spirits are submitting to him."
News of Jesus spread rapidly throughout Galilee. Jesus heals many people.
Jesus and his disciples left the temple. They went straight along with James and John to the house of Simon and Andrew.
Once his wife was lying in bed. She had a fever. They told Jesus about her.
He walked over to her. He took her hand and helped her up. The fever had left her. Then she began to serve them.
This night, after sunset, the people brought to Jesus all who were sick. And they also brought all who were possessed by demons.
All the people in the city gathered at the gate.
Jesus healed many of them. They had all kinds of diseases. He also cast out many demons. But He did not allow the demons to speak, because they knew who He was. Jesus prays in a quiet place
It was very early in the morning and still dark. Issa got up and left the house. He went to a place where he could be alone. There he prayed.
Simon and his friends went to meet Jesus.
When they found him, they shouted, "Everyone is looking for you!"
Jesus replied, "Let's go to another place. I want to go to the nearby towns. I must preach there too. That's why I came."
He walked throughout Galilee. He was preaching in their synagogues. And he was casting out demons as well. Jesus heals a man with a skin disease
A man with a skin disease came to Jesus. Kneeling down, he begged Jesus. He told him, "If you are willing, you can make me 'clean'. You can do it."
Jesus was filled with deep concern. He reached out his hand and touched the man. "I am willing to do it," he said. "Purify yourself!"
He recovered from the illness. He was cured.
Jesus sent him away immediately. He gave him a strong warning.
"Don't tell this to anyone," he said. "Go and show yourself to the priest. Make the offerings that Moses commanded. It will be a testimony to the priest and to the people that you are 'clean.'"
The man went out and started telling the story immediately. He spread the news to everyone. That's why Jesus could no longer enter the city openly. He stayed outside in deserted places. But people still came to him from everywhere.
Jesus heals a man who cannot walk.
A few days later, Jesus returned to the entrance of Capernaum. The people heard that he had returned home.
Many people gathered, and there was no more room. Even outside the door, there was no more room. And Jesus taught the word to them.
Four of those who came were carrying a man who could not walk.
Because they could not get near Jesus through the crowd, they made an opening in the roof above Jesus. Then they lowered the man on his stretcher through the opening.
Jesus saw their faith. He said to the man, "Son, your sins are forgiven."
Some of the teachers of the law were sitting there. And they were reasoning within themselves,
Why does this man speak like this? He's saying something very evil! Thank God that He forgives sins!
Immediately, Jesus knew what they were thinking. He said to them, "Why do you think such things in your hearts?"
Is it easier to say to this man, "Your sins are forgiven"? or to say, "Rise, take up your mat and walk"?
I love you, you know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins. Then Jesus said to the man who could not walk.
"I tell you," he said, "get up. Take your mat and go home."
The man stood up and took his bed. Then he walked away while everyone was watching. All the people were amazed. They praised God and said, "We have never seen anything like this!" Jesus Chooses Levi
Once again, Jesus went out to the side of the Sea of Galilee. A large crowd gathered around him. He began to preach to them.
As he was walking, he saw Levi, the son of Alphaeus. Levi was sitting in the tax booth. "Follow me," Jesus said to him. Levi got up and followed him.
After this, Jesus was having dinner at the house of Levi. Many tax collectors and "sinners" were eating with him and his disciples. They were part of the large crowd that followed Jesus.
Some of the teachers of the law who were Pharisees were there. They saw Jesus eating with "sinners" and tax collectors. So they asked his disciples, "Why does he eat with tax collectors and 'sinners'?"
Jesus heard this. And he said to them: "Those who are in good health do not need a physician, but those who are ill do. I did not come to call the righteous to repentance and to follow me, but I came to call sinners to repentance and to follow me." Jesus is asked about fasting.
John's disciples and the Pharisees used to fast, and they would not eat. Some people came to Jesus and said to him, "John's disciples fast, and the disciples of the Pharisees also fast, but your disciples do not fast. Why do your disciples not fast?"
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom fast while he is with them? As long as they have the bridegroom with them, they cannot fast."
When the groom's party arrives, they take something from them. On that day, they go without eating.
People do not sew a new piece of cloth onto old clothes. If they did that, the new piece would pull away from the old. This would make the tear even worse.
People don't put new wine into old wineskins. If they do, the wine will burst the wineskins. Then the wine and the wineskins will both be ruined. No, everyone who puts new wine into old wineskins is foolish. The wine will burst the wineskins, spilling the wine and destroying the wineskins. Instead, new wine must be put into new wineskins.
One Sabbath, Jesus was walking with his disciples through the grainfields. The disciples began to pick some of the heads of grain.
The Pharisees said to Jesus, "Look! This is a violation of the law for you to do on the Sabbath. Why do your disciples do it?"
Answer, "Have you never read what David did? He and his men were hungry. They needed food."
In those days Abiathar was the high priest. David went into the tent of the Lord and ate the consecrated bread. Only the priests were allowed to eat it. David even gave some of it to his men.
Then Jesus said to them, "The Sabbath was made for man, and not man for the Sabbath."
Therefore, the Son of Man is Lord even on the day of rest.
Healing on the Sabbath.
Once again, Jesus entered the synagogue. There was a man there who had a withered and deformed hand.
Some of the Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. They were watching him closely. They would love to see if he was going to heal the man on the Sabbath day.
Jesus said to the man with the withered and deformed hand, "Rise to your feet in full view of everyone," He said.
Then Jesus asked them, "What does the law say we must do on the Sabbath? Is it to do good or to do evil? Is it to save a life or to destroy it?" But no one answered him.
Jesus looked at them, angry. He was very upset because their hearts were stubborn. Then He said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." He stretched it out, and his hand was completely restored, just like new.
And after that, the Pharisees went out and began to plot with the Herodians against Jesus, hoping to kill him. The crowds followed Jesus.
Jesus walked to the Sea of Galilee with his disciples. A large crowd from Galilee followed him.
People heard all the things Jesus was doing. Many of them came to him. They came from Judea, Jerusalem, and from the region of Ammon. They came from the territories east of the Jordan River. And they came from the region around Tyre and Sidon.
Because of the crowd, Jesus told his disciples to prepare a small boat for him. This way, the people would not crowd him.
Jesus healed many people. That's why sick people would push themselves to touch him.
When evil spirits saw him, they fell down before him. The spirits cried out, "You are the Son of God!"
Jesus commanded them not to tell who he was. Jesus appoints the twelve apostles.
Jesus went up on a mountain. He called some people to come to him, and they came.
He appointed 12 of them and called them disciples. From then on, they were with him. He also sent them out to preach.
They had the authority to cast out demons.
Why did Jesus appoint the twelve. Simon was one of them. Jesus called him Peter.
They were James, the son of Zebedee, and his brother John. Jesus gave them the name Boanerges. Boanerges means Sons of Thunder.
They were also Andrew, Philip, Bartholomew, Matthew, Thomas, and James the son of Alphaeus. And they were Thaddaeus and Simon the Canaanite.
Judas Iscariot was one of them too. He was the one who would later betray Jesus to his enemies. Jesus and Belial.
Jesus entered a house. Once again a large crowd gathered. It was so large that Jesus and his disciples couldn't even eat.
His family heard this story. They went to take him and take care of him. They said about him, "He is crazy."
Some of the teachers on the law were there, who had come down from Jerusalem. And they said, "He is possessed by Beelzebul! He is driving out demons by the prince of demons."
So Jesus called out to them and spoke to them with parables. He said to them, "How can Satan cast out Satan?"
If a kingdom is fighting with its own soul, it cannot stand.
If the family is scattered, it cannot endure.
